---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Project from "../components/Project.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";

const posts = (await getCollection("writing"))
  .filter((p) => !p.data.draft)
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.pubDate.valueOf() - a.data.pubDate.valueOf());

const fmt = (d: Date) =>
  d.toLocaleDateString("en-AU", { year: "numeric", month: "short" });
---

<Base
  title="David Taing — Software Engineer"
  description="Software engineer in Sydney working in Elixir, Phoenix and TypeScript on a production healthcare platform, plus side projects measured against pre-registered criteria."
>
  <section class="pt-6 pb-4">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-widest text-ink-3 uppercase">Software engineer &middot; Sydney</p>
    <h1 class="mt-5 font-serif text-4xl leading-[1.08] font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance text-ink sm:text-5xl">
      I build production systems, then check whether they actually work.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
      I work in Elixir, Phoenix and TypeScript on a production healthcare platform, mostly on the
      parts where correctness is hard to see: evaluation harnesses, instrumentation, and the failure
      paths nobody exercises until they matter. Alongside it I run side projects against
      pre-registered criteria, which means some of them get closed on purpose.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2 font-mono text-xs tracking-wide">
      <a href="https://github.com/davidtaing" class="text-ink-3 hover:text-accent">GitHub</a>
      <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/teeang/" class="text-ink-3 hover:text-accent">LinkedIn</a>
      <a href="mailto:dave@teeang.net" class="text-ink-3 hover:text-accent">dave@teeang.net</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="work" class="mt-20 scroll-mt-6">
    <h2 class="font-mono text-xs tracking-widest text-ink-3 uppercase">Currently</h2>
    <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3">
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Medi Apps</h3>
      <span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-3">Fullstack Engineer &middot; Dec 2025 to now</span>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] text-ink-3">
      AI-assisted clinical workflows on a production healthcare platform. Elixir, Phoenix, LiveView,
      TypeScript.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
      <li class="border-l-2 border-line pl-4">
        Built the evaluation harness that makes prompt and model changes regression-testable before
        release, calibrating automated judges against a hand-labelled reference set. Retired a
        shipped quality metric once a sensitivity probe showed it could not discriminate.
      </li>
      <li class="border-l-2 border-line pl-4">
        Took ownership of time from consult to usable note, an uninstrumented metric nobody owned.
        Built the measurement first, then traced the pipeline end to end and fixed several
        independent sources of delay rather than optimising one in isolation.
      </li>
      <li class="border-l-2 border-line pl-4">
        Moved production prompts into runtime prompt management, designing the failure path in so no
        caller can bypass it. Sequenced the rollout by ascending stakes, so the earliest failures
        were the cheapest. A colleague reused the pattern on a new surface.
      </li>
      <li class="border-l-2 border-line pl-4">
        Instrumented the platform behind a shared telemetry facade, with a label policy that keeps
        identifiers and free text out of metrics, and a CI check that derives the legal metric set
        from the running instrumentation so dashboards cannot drift from the code.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="projects" class="mt-20 scroll-mt-6">
    <h2 class="font-mono text-xs tracking-widest text-ink-3 uppercase">Projects</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
      Each of these ran as a short, concentrated build followed by a decision. The state is the part
      worth reading: a project going quiet and a project being closed on purpose are different
      events, and only one of them is a result.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8">
      <Project name="snag" state="killed" stack="TypeScript · Elixir">
        <p>
          FMEA-style failure-mode analysis for pull requests, turning a spec or a diff into a
          reviewable failure map. Run as a skill inside a coding agent, driving the agent's read-only
          tools.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          I ran it against eleven pre-registered experiments with criteria frozen before each run.
          One supported the core claim, two came back refuted, and the experiment testing the graph
          artifact itself landed inconclusive. So I closed the product in writing, recorded the
          revive trigger, and published the fault-injection bench behind the headline result as a
          reproducibility artifact.
        </p>
      </Project>

      <Project
        name="latchkey"
        state="parked"
        stack="Elixir · Ash · Commanded"
        href="https://github.com/snag-run/latchkey"
      >
        <p>
          Models a residential tenancy using event sourcing and domain-driven design, drawing on four
          years working in property management before I moved into engineering. Reconstructs a
          tenancy's full history from an append-only log, complete and tamper-evident enough to stand
          as evidence at a tribunal hearing.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Twelve architecture decision records, cited by section from the modules they govern, and an
          85 percent coverage floor enforced by the same alias locally and in CI.
        </p>
      </Project>

      <Project
        name="treetop"
        state="shipped"
        stack="Go"
        href="https://github.com/snag-run/treetop"
      >
        <p>
          A top-style live tracker for git worktrees across every project on a machine: the branch,
          which worktrees have an active agent session, and the PR and CI status of each, with quick
          links. Supports Claude Code and Codex.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">Complete, released, and carrying a test file for essentially every source file.</p>
      </Project>

      <Project
        name="bluehex"
        state="active"
        stack="Next.js · TypeScript · Supabase"
        href="https://github.com/codesydney/bluehex"
      >
        <p>
          I lead the commercial arm of the Code.Sydney community: an open-source directory where
          Claude and Anthropic practitioners publish a profile and have the credentials behind it
          verified.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Six outside contributors across three continents landed merged work in the first two weeks,
          most finding the repo cold and shipping without direct onboarding. I scope issues to
          contributor experience level, review incoming work, and own delivery.
        </p>
      </Project>
    </div>
  </section>

  {
    posts.length > 0 && (
      <section id="writing" class="mt-20 scroll-mt-6">
        <h2 class="font-mono text-xs tracking-widest text-ink-3 uppercase">Writing</h2>
        <ul class="mt-5">
          {posts.map((post) => (
            <li class="border-t border-line py-5">
              <a href={`/writing/${post.id}`} class="group block">
                <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3">
                  <h3 class="font-serif text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink group-hover:text-accent">
                    {post.data.title}
                  </h3>
                  <span class="ml-auto font-mono text-xs text-ink-3">{fmt(post.data.pubDate)}</span>
                </div>
                <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
                  {post.data.description}
                </p>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>
    )
  }
</Base>
